userStories.description:
**Goal** — Add reusable batch readers for principal display names and ticket ref/title labels without changing existing consumers yet.

**Motivation** — The dashboard spans projects, while the web's name lookups (`useProjectMemberNames`, `useProjectAgentNames`) are per project, so the API has to send the names.

**Approach** — New `apps/api/src/common/utils/event-labels.ts`. It takes the Prisma client as an argument and imports only the `PrismaClient` type, as `common/utils/advisory-lock.ts` does; it is called only from `*.repository.ts` files (`prisma-home.repository.ts`, `prisma-timeline.repository.ts`, `prisma-fleet-job-labels.repository.ts`), so Prisma access stays in the repository layer:

- Both functions drop duplicate and empty-string ids, and make no query when nothing is left.
- `readPrincipalNames` issues one `user.findMany({ where: { id: { in: ids } }, select: { id, name, email } })` and one `agent.findMany({ where: { id: { in: ids } }, select: { id, name } })`. An id found in neither table is absent from the map.
- `readTicketLabels` issues one `ticket.findMany({ where: { id: { in: ids } }, select: { id, number, title, project: { select: { key: true } } } })`. The ref is `${project.key}-${number}`.

The select notation above names selected fields; implement Prisma select flags as `true`. Mirror the user's trimmed name/email-local-part and agent-name precedent, but do not copy that reader's missing-principal prose. A soft-deleted agent keeps its name. A ticket id that no longer exists has no map entry; soft-deleted tickets are deliberately included. Errors propagate normally with no catch.

**Scope** — In: the two new utility functions and their co-located fake-client unit tests. Out: home/timeline/fleet wiring belongs to US-002/US-003/US-004; DTOs, generated contracts and web rendering belong to downstream stories. No existing module or test needs modification for this story.

**Interface** —
```ts
import type { PrismaClient } from '../../generated/prisma/client';

export type EventLabelClient = Pick<PrismaClient, 'user' | 'agent' | 'ticket'>;

export interface TicketLabel { ref: string; title: string }

/** Display names by principal id: a user's trimmed name, else the email local part; an agent's name. Never an email. */
export async function readPrincipalNames(client: EventLabelClient, ids: readonly string[]): Promise<Map<string, string>>;

/** `KEY-N` ref and title by ticket id. Soft-deleted tickets are included: an event about a deleted ticket still names it. */
export async function readTicketLabels(client: EventLabelClient, ids: readonly string[]): Promise<Map<string, TicketLabel>>;
```

**Tests** — Write `apps/api/src/common/utils/event-labels.spec.ts` first, using fake `user`, `agent` and `ticket` delegates whose findMany methods are vi spies. Assert the real functions' returned maps and query arguments, including duplicate/empty ids and no-query empty input. No database is needed.
